  1	//! `paste` — the clipboard's half of Cut / Copy / Paste (spec §6, table
  2	//! "Clips").
  3	//!
  4	//! Its own module because pasting is a different concern from every other
  5	//! placement op: the material does not come from the project. A `move_clip`
  6	//! or a `duplicate` rearranges clips the project already owns, so the ids,
  7	//! the media and the tracks all exist; a paste arrives as JSON that may have
  8	//! been written by ANOTHER document — a different id space, a different
  9	//! media list, a different frame grid, possibly a different canvas — and has
 10	//! to be made to fit before a single clip lands.
 11	//!
 12	//! ### The payload
 13	//!
 14	//! ```json
 15	//! {"clips": [ …the §5.2 clip objects… ], "media": [ …the §5.2 media rows… ],
 16	//!  "nests": [ …the §5.2 nest objects the clips show… ]}
 17	//! ```
 18	//!
 19	//! It is exactly what `EditorViewController+Clipboard.swift` puts on
 20	//! `NSPasteboard.general` under `com.kgalligan.framejob.clips`: the clips
 21	//! copied verbatim out of the source document's own `project.json`, plus the
 22	//! media rows those clips name and any nest they show. Four things about it
 23	//! are worth stating before the code:
 24	//!
 25	//! 1. **A payload clip's `track` is a DEPTH, not an id** — 0 is the
 26	//!    front-most track the copy covered, 1 the one behind it, and so on. A
 27	//!    track id from another document means nothing here, and the depth is
 28	//!    the only thing about the source's track stack worth carrying: it is
 29	//!    what makes a two-lane selection paste back onto two lanes.
 30	//! 2. **Ids are re-minted, always.** Clips, actions, any media row the
 31	//!    project does not already have, and every nest — with its tracks, its
 32	//!    clips and their actions — get fresh ids from `next_id`, so a payload
 33	//!    can be pasted repeatedly and into the document it came from.
 34	//! 3. **A nest is COPIED, never matched.** Media is matched by path because
 35	//!    it names a file two documents can share; a nest is content, and two
 36	//!    nests that look alike are no more the same object than two clips are.
 37	//!    So every paste of a nest clip brings its own copy, and editing one
 38	//!    afterwards leaves the other alone.
 39	//! 4. **Unknown keys are ignored**, deliberately: no `deny_unknown_fields`
 40	//!    here. The payload is machine-written, a future build will add keys to
 41	//!    it, and refusing a whole paste over a key this build has not learned
 42	//!    yet would be worse than dropping it — the same reasoning `model.rs`
 43	//!    gives for the project schema itself.
 44	//!
 45	//! ### Making it fit
 46	//!
 47	//! Every project time is re-snapped to THIS document's frame grid
 48	//! ([`Project::snapped`]), because the payload may come from a document at
 49	//! another rate; source times are not, for the same reason `trim_start` and
 50	//! `split` do not snap them — an `in` point is measured on the media's grid,
 51	//! not the canvas's. Media is matched to the existing `media[]` **by path**,
 52	//! which is `add_media`'s one-entry-per-path rule, so pasting between two
 53	//! documents that both reference `/m/talk.mov` reuses the row that is there
 54	//! and pasting into a document that has never seen it brings the row along;
 55	//! a row brought along first passes `add_media`'s field checks, with its
 56	//! `original` stored as `add_media` stores it, and a row matched by path is
 57	//! the project's own and is not checked. Every group of every clip that
 58	//! lands, nested ones included, first passes the patch path's
 59	//! normalise-and-check, as `paste_attributes`' do — a video group
 60	//! `set_video`'s own chain, `Project::settle_video`.
 61	//!
 62	//! Placement obeys the overlap rule like everything else (spec §11), and a
 63	//! clip the rule refuses on its target track goes on a NEW track in front —
 64	//! the same fallback `EditorViewController+AddClips.swift` uses for Add
 65	//! Text, and for the same reason: a paste at a busy playhead should land
 66	//! somewhere visible rather than beep. That fallback is why the only
 67	//! "changes nothing" answer here is an empty clip list.
 68	//!
 69	//! `paste_attributes` reads the same payload and places nothing; it is
 70	//! `edit_paste_attributes.rs`'s, which parses the payload through
 71	//! [`parse_payload`].
